msgid "Configuring the ValidatorFactory"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Sometimes, you might need to configure the behavior of the "
"`ValidatorFactory`, for instance to use a specific `ParameterNameProvider`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"While the `ValidatorFactory` is instantiated by Quarkus itself,\n"
"you can very easily tweak it by declaring replacement beans that will be "
"injected in the configuration."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you create a bean of the following types in your application, it will "
"automatically be injected into the `ValidatorFactory` configuration:"
msgstr ""

msgid "`javax.validation.ClockProvider`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`javax.validation.ConstraintValidator`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`javax.validation.ConstraintValidatorFactory`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`javax.validation.MessageInterpolator`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`javax.validation.ParameterNameProvider`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`javax.validation.TraversableResolver`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`org.hibernate.validator.spi.properties.GetterPropertySelectionStrategy`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`org.hibernate.validator.spi.nodenameprovider.PropertyNodeNameProvider`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`org.hibernate.validator.spi.scripting.ScriptEvaluatorFactory`"
msgstr ""

msgid "You don't have to wire anything."
msgstr ""

msgid "Obviously, for each listed type, you can declare only one bean."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Most of the time, these beans should be declared as `@ApplicationScoped`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"However, in the case of ``ConstraintValidator``s that are dependent of "
"attributes of the constraint annotation\n"
"(typically when implementing the `initialize(A constraintAnnotation)` "
"method),\n"
"use the `@Dependent` scope to make sure each annotation context has a "
"separate instance of the `ConstraintValidator` bean."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If customizing the `ValidatorFactory` through the available configuration "
"properties and the CDI beans above doesn't cover your requirements,\n"
"you can customize it further by registering `ValidatorFactoryCustomizer` "
"beans."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For instance, you could override the built-in validator enforcing the "
"`@Email` constraint and use `MyEmailValidator` instead with the following "
"class:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"All beans implementing `ValidatorFactoryCustomizer` are applied, meaning you "
"can have several of them.\n"
"If you need to enforce some ordering, you can use the usual `@javax."
"annotation.Priority` annotation - beans with higher priority are applied "
"first."
msgstr ""

msgid "Constraint validators as beans"
msgstr ""

msgid "You can declare your constraint validators as CDI beans:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When initializing a constraint validator of a given type,\n"
"Quarkus will check if a bean of this type is available and, if so, it will "
"use it instead of instantiating a `ConstraintValidator`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Thus, as demonstrated in our example, you can fully use injection in your "
"constraint validator beans."
msgstr ""

msgid "The scope you choose for your `ConstraintValidator` bean is important:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If the same instance of the `ConstraintValidator` bean can be used across "
"the whole application, use the `@ApplicationScoped` scope."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If the `ConstraintValidator` bean implements the `initialize(A "
"constraintAnnotation)` method and depends on the state of the constraint "
"annotation, use the `@Dependent` scope to make sure each annotation context "
"has a separate and properly configured instance of the `ConstraintValidator` "
"bean."
msgstr ""

msgid "Validation and localization"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"By default, constraint violation messages will be returned in the build "
"system locale."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can configure this behavior by adding the following configuration in "
"your `application.properties`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you are using RESTEasy Reactive or RESTEasy Classic, in the context of a "
"JAX-RS endpoint,\n"
"Hibernate Validator will automatically resolve the optimal locale to use "
"from the `Accept-Language` HTTP header,\n"
"provided the supported locales have been properly specified in the "
"`application.properties`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A similar mechanism exists for GraphQL services based on the `quarkus-"
"smallrye-graphql` extension."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If this default mechanism is not sufficient and you need a custom locale "
"resolution, you can add additional ``org.hibernate.validator.spi."
"messageinterpolation.LocaleResolver``s:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Any CDI bean implementing `org.hibernate.validator.spi.messageinterpolation."
"LocaleResolver` will be taken into account."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The ``LocaleResolver``s are consulted in the order of `@Priority` (higher "
"priority goes first)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A `LocaleResolver` may return null if it cannot resolve the locale, it will "
"then be ignored."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The first non-null locale returned by a `LocaleResolver` is the one resolved."
""
msgstr ""

msgid "Validation groups for REST endpoint or service method validation"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It's sometimes necessary to enable different validation constraints\n"
"for the same class when it's passed to a different method."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For example, a `Book` may need to have a `null` identifier when passed to "
"the `post` method\n"
"(because the identifier will be generated),\n"
"but a non-`null` identifier when passed to the `put` method\n"
"(because the method needs the identifier to know what to update)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To address this, you can take advantage of validation groups.\n"
"Validation groups are markers that you put on your constraints in order to "
"enable or disable them at will."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"First, define the `Post` and `Put` groups, which are just Java interfaces."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Make the custom groups extend the `Default` group.\n"
"This means that whenever these groups are enabled, the `Default` group is "
"also enabled.\n"
"This is useful if you have constraints that you want validated in both the "
"`Post` and `Put` method:\n"
"you can simply use the default group on those constraints, like on the "
"`title` property below."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Then add the relevant constraints to `Book`, assigning the right group to "
"each constraint:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Finally, add a `@ConvertGroup` annotation next to your `@Valid` annotation "
"in your validated method."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Enable the `Post` group, meaning only constraints assigned to the `Post` "
"(and `Default`) groups\n"
"will be validated for the `book` parameter of the `post` method.\n"
"In this case, it means `Book.id` must be `null` and `Book.title` must not be "
"blank."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Enable the `Put` group, meaning only constraints assigned to the `Put` (and "
"`Default`) groups\n"
"will be validated for the `book` parameter of the `put` method.\n"
"In this case, it means `Book.id` must not be `null` and `Book.title` must "
"not be blank."
msgstr ""

